You are working on FitForge AI, an AI-powered fitness platform.

Stack:
- Next.js 14+ App Router, TypeScript, src/ directory
- shadcn/ui + Tailwind for components
- MongoDB + Mongoose for persistence
- Auth: NextAuth (credentials + optional Google)
- AI providers: Gemini (gemini-3.6-flash, 6 rotating keys) and NVIDIA NIM
  (nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b for text/reasoning,
   meta/llama-3.2-11b-vision-instruct for any image/vision task),
  called through an internal orchestrator — never call provider SDKs
  directly from components or route handlers.
- External data: ExerciseDB V1 (exercise catalog/GIFs), USDA FoodData
  Central (nutrition), Open Food Facts (packaged/barcode foods)

Build methodology — MODULE BY MODULE, not layer by layer:
1. Before writing any schema or route for a module, read every UI file
   that module touches (components/views, relevant modals, relevant
   common components) and list every field, prop, and mock-data shape
   it references. That list is the source of truth for the schema —
   don't add speculative fields the UI doesn't use, and don't leave out
   fields the UI clearly needs.
2. Build the Mongoose schema + Zod validation, the API routes, and the
   UI wiring for that module in the same task, then confirm the build
   is clean before moving to the next module.
3. Never edit files belonging to a module you weren't asked to work on
   in the current task — this is what keeps modules mergeable in
   parallel without conflicts.

Non-negotiable architecture rules:
1. All deterministic numbers (BMI, BMR, TDEE, calorie/macro math, volume,
   progress %) are computed by our own TypeScript functions in
   src/lib/calculations — never by an AI model.
2. AI never invents exercise IDs or food nutrition values. AI proposes by
   name/intent; the backend resolves those against our own exercises /
   foods collections and only persists validated IDs.
3. Every AI call must request structured JSON validated against a Zod
   schema before it's trusted or saved. Reject and retry (or fall back
   to the next provider) on schema failure.
4. API keys are server-only env vars, read only inside src/lib and
   src/app/api. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_*.
5. UI must visually distinguish "Measured", "Calculated", and
   "AI Recommendation" data using the small badges already defined in
   the design (OriginBadge component).
6. AI must not produce medical diagnoses, medication guidance, or
   extreme-diet content. If body metrics look concerning, the copy
   should suggest seeing a healthcare professional rather than the
   model interpreting the finding itself.
7. Route access must match the PUBLIC/PRIVATE map below at all times —
   check src/middleware.ts before adding any new route.

Don't ask me to re-paste context — read this file and the current repo
state before proposing changes. Work in small, reviewable commits per
module/task, not one giant diff.

Keep this table current in src/middleware.ts as routes are added — every phase below must respect it, not just Phase 0.
PUBLIC (no session required):
  /                    landing page
  /login
  /register
  /forgot-password
  /reset-password
  /api/auth/*          (NextAuth + register/forgot/reset handlers)

SEMI-PROTECTED (session required, profile NOT yet complete):
  /onboarding
  /api/onboarding

PRIVATE (session required AND onboarding complete):
  /dashboard
  /workouts, /workouts/[id], /workouts/active/[id]
  /exercises, /exercises/[id]
  /nutrition
  /progress
  /ai-coach
  /profile
  /settings
  /api/* (everything except the PUBLIC and SEMI-PROTECTED routes above)

Middleware behavior:
  - No session + hitting a SEMI-PROTECTED or PRIVATE route -> redirect /login
  - Session but onboarding incomplete + hitting a PRIVATE route -> redirect /onboarding
  - Session + onboarding complete + hitting /login, /register, or /onboarding
    -> redirect /dashboard


Set up the shared infrastructure every module depends on, so modules
can later be built independently without redefining contracts:

1. src/lib/db/mongodb.ts — cached Mongoose connection helper safe for
   Next.js hot reload / serverless.
2. src/middleware.ts implementing the ROUTE ACCESS MAP above exactly,
   reading the NextAuth session token and an `onboardingComplete` flag
   on the user/profile.
3. src/types/ — shared domain interfaces (User, Profile, Exercise,
   WorkoutPlan, WorkoutSession, FoodItem, FoodLog, BodyMeasurement,
   AIPlan, etc.) that BOTH the API routes and the UI components import
   from. This is the shared contract that prevents modules from
   drifting apart when built in parallel.
4. src/lib/api/response.ts — a helper enforcing one response envelope
   everywhere: { data } on success, { error: { message, code } } on
   failure, with correct HTTP status codes.
5. src/lib/validation/base.ts — shared Zod primitives (objectId string,
   ISO date, pagination query params) that per-module schemas extend.
6. src/lib/calculations/ — empty module folder with function signatures
   stubbed (bmi, bmr, tdee, macroTargets, workoutVolume, progressDelta)
   and full TypeScript types, so module phases can import against a
   stable interface even before Phase 5/7 fill in the real math.
7. src/lib/ai/orchestrator.ts — provider clients wired (Gemini pool,
   NVIDIA client) with a working retry/fallback chain, but NO prompts
   or schemas yet — those get added per-module in Phases 5, 6, 7, 8.
8. NextAuth basic config (credentials provider, session callback
   exposing userId + onboardingComplete) — full register/login UI
   wiring happens in Phase 2.

Confirm `npm run build` is clean before moving to Phase 1.